The Only Kubernetes & Docker Commands You Need When Everything’s on Fire:

👉 Container Crisis Mode:
- docker ps -a | grep Exited - Show stopped containers
- kubectl get pods –field-selector=status.phase=Failed - Show failed pods
- docker logs -f –tail 100 {container} - Follow container logs
- kubectl describe pod {name} - Show pod details and events
- docker system prune -a - Remove unused containers and images

👉 Debug Warriors:
- kubectl exec -it {pod} – /bin/bash - Access pod shell
- docker exec -it {container} sh - Access container shell
- kubectl port-forward {pod} 8080:80 - Forward pod port to local
- docker inspect {container} | grep -i ip - Get container IP address
- kubectl logs {pod} -c {container} –previous - Show previous container logs

👉 Resource Rangers:
- kubectl top nodes - Show node CPU and memory usage
- kubectl top pods - Show pod resource usage
- docker stats –no-stream - Show container resource usage
- kubectl get pods -o wide - Show pods with node and IP info
- docker images –format “table {{.Repository}}\t{{.Tag}}\t{{.Size}}” - List images with size

👉 Network Ninjas:
- kubectl get svc - List services
- docker network ls - List networks
- kubectl get ingress - List ingress rules
- docker port {container} - Show port mappings
- kubectl get endpoints - Show service endpoints

👉 Quick Fixes:
- kubectl delete pod {name} –force –grace-period=0 - Force delete pod
- docker restart {container} - Restart container
- kubectl rollout restart deployment/{name} - Restart deployment
- docker rm $(docker ps -aq) - Remove all containers
- kubectl scale deployment {name} –replicas=0 - Scale deployment to zero

👉 YAML Wranglers:
- kubectl apply -f - | kubectl delete -f - - Apply then delete from stdin
- kubectl get pod {name} -o yaml > debug.yaml - Export pod config to file
- docker run –rm -it {image} sh - Run temporary container
- kubectl create secret generic –dry-run=client -o yaml - Generate secret YAML
- kubectl explain {resource} - Show resource documentation

👉 Cluster Intelligence:
- kubectl get all -A - Show all resources in all namespaces
- kubectl cluster-info - Show cluster info
- docker version - Show Docker version
- kubectl config current-context - Show current cluster context
- kubectl get events –sort-by=’.lastTimestamp’ - Show recent cluster events

👉 Pro Tips:
- alias k=kubectl - Create kubectl shortcut
- kubectl config set-context –current –namespace={ns} - Switch namespace
- docker system df - Show Docker disk usage
- kubectl api-resources - List all available resources
- watch kubectl get pods - Monitor pods in real-time
